"""Foundations: Hilbert space, old quantum theory, quantum mechanics, its mathematical formulation, quantum differential calculus, transformation theory, observables, self-adjoint operators, eigenvalues, operator theory, quantum logic, von Neumann algebras, commutators and the uncertainty principle."""

NARRATIVES = {
    "hilbert_space": [
        ("p", r"A particle between two hard walls a distance $L$ apart is prepared with the same amplitude at every point of the box. A measurement of its energy then returns the lowest level in a definite fraction of runs. Computing that fraction uses the two properties that define a Hilbert space: an inner product and completeness."),
        ("p", r"The states of the particle are square-integrable functions on the interval. The inner product of two of them gives an amplitude, and its squared modulus gives the probability of finding one prepared state in another. The energy eigenfunctions $\phi_n(x)=\sqrt{2/L}\,\sin(n\pi x/L)$ are orthonormal, and every state is a convergent sum of them:"),
        ("eq", r"""\langle\phi|\psi\rangle=\int_0^L\phi^*(x)\psi(x)\,dx,\qquad
\psi=\sum_{n\geq1}c_n\phi_n,\\
c_n=\langle\phi_n|\psi\rangle,\qquad
\sum_{n\geq1}|c_n|^2=\langle\psi|\psi\rangle=1."""),
        ("p", r"For the flat state $\psi=1/\sqrt L$ the coefficients are $c_n=\sqrt2\,[1-(-1)^n]/(n\pi)$. The even terms vanish because the flat state is symmetric about the centre of the box, while the even eigenfunctions are antisymmetric. The odd terms give"),
        ("eq", r"""P(E_1)=|c_1|^2=\frac{8}{\pi^2}\approx0.811,\qquad
P(E_n)=\frac{8}{n^2\pi^2}\quad(n\ \text{odd})."""),
        ("p", r"The probabilities add up to one because the sum of $1/n^2$ over odd $n$ equals $\pi^2/8$. This is Parseval's identity. It holds because the space is complete: a sequence of finite superpositions whose members approach each other in mean square converges to a state inside the space. The probability missing after the terms up to $n=N$ falls roughly as $4/(\pi^2N)$ for large $N$. The partial sums vanish at the walls themselves and overshoot the flat value by 18 percent next to them, however many terms are kept. Convergence in the norm is nevertheless the physically relevant sense, because the norm controls every probability."),
        ("p", r"The same state has infinite mean energy. With $E_n=n^2\pi^2\hbar^2/(2mL^2)$, every odd term of the energy sum contributes the same amount:"),
        ("eq", r"""\langle H\rangle=\sum_{n\ \text{odd}}\frac{8}{n^2\pi^2}\,
\frac{n^2\pi^2\hbar^2}{2mL^2}
=\sum_{n\ \text{odd}}\frac{4\hbar^2}{mL^2}=\infty."""),
        ("p", r"The flat wave function belongs to the Hilbert space, but not to the domain of the Hamiltonian. It jumps at the walls, where every eigenfunction vanishes, and an abrupt step needs arbitrarily large momentum components. The divergence comes from the step itself, so walls of finite height do not remove it. A preparation with finite energy must round the edges over some length, and that length cuts off the sum."),
        ("p", r"The Hilbert space is not physical space. For one particle in three dimensions it is a space of wave functions of three coordinates; for $N$ particles the functions depend on $3N$ coordinates. A single spin needs only the two-dimensional space $\mathbb C^2$, and $N$ spins need dimension $2^N$. A finite set of box modes suffices for questions about low energies, but not for the mean energy of a state with sharp edges. The distinction between the space and an operator's domain recurs in spectral theory and in the treatment of self-adjoint operators."),
    ],
    "old_quantum_theory": [
        ("p", r"Hydrogen emits light at sharp wavelengths, among them the red Balmer line at 656 nanometres. Between 1913 and 1925 these lines were explained by selecting particular classical orbits, before wave mechanics existed. The selection rule of that period, Bohr--Sommerfeld quantization, worked for hydrogen and for the oscillator, and its failures show what the later theory had to change."),
        ("p", r"The rule applies to motion that separates into periodic coordinates $q_k$ with conjugate momenta $p_k$. The action integral over one period of each coordinate must be an integer multiple of Planck's constant $h$:"),
        ("eq", r"""J_k=\oint p_k\,dq_k=n_k h,\qquad n_k=0,1,2,\ldots"""),
        ("p", r"For an oscillator of mass $m$ and angular frequency $\omega$, the orbit at energy $E$ is an ellipse in phase space with area $2\pi E/\omega$. The rule gives $E_n=n\hbar\omega$, with the spacing $\hbar\omega$ seen in molecular vibrational ladders. The ground energy is wrong, however. Wave mechanics gives $(n+\tfrac12)\hbar\omega$, and isotope shifts in molecular band spectra, analysed by Mulliken in 1924 and 1925, already required the half quantum. The modern semiclassical rule replaces $n$ by $n+\tfrac12$ for motion between two smooth turning points. That correction comes from the phase of the wave at the turning points, which an orbit cannot supply."),
        ("p", r"For hydrogen, write $k=e^2/(4\pi\varepsilon_0)$ for the Coulomb strength and use circular orbits. Quantized angular momentum $mvr=n\hbar$ together with the force balance $mv^2/r=k/r^2$ gives"),
        ("eq", r"""r_n=\frac{n^2\hbar^2}{mk}=n^2a_0,\qquad
E_n=-\frac{mk^2}{2\hbar^2n^2}=-\frac{13.6\ \text{eV}}{n^2}."""),
        ("p", r"Here $a_0=52.9$ picometres is the Bohr radius. The transition from $n=3$ to $n=2$, with the reduced-mass correction, gives a vacuum wavelength of 656.5 nanometres. Sommerfeld's elliptic orbits add a radial action. The sum of the radial and angular actions depends only on the energy, so the levels depend only on $n=n_r+n_\varphi$. This reproduces the degeneracy of the Kepler problem:"),
        ("eq", r"""J_r+J_\varphi=2\pi k\sqrt{\frac{m}{-2E}}=(n_r+n_\varphi)h."""),
        ("p", r"For large $n$ the spacing of neighbouring levels, divided by $h$, approaches the classical orbital frequency $mk^2/(2\pi\hbar^3n^3)$. Bohr used this correspondence in 1913 to fix the constant in his rule, requiring large orbits to radiate at their classical frequency. Sommerfeld's relativistic treatment of the elliptic orbits gave a fine-structure formula that agrees with the later Dirac result. Semiclassical analysis of the Dirac equation, including the precession of the spin, helps explain that agreement."),
        ("p", r"The failures are specific. The rule requires $n_\varphi\geq1$, because an orbit with zero angular momentum would pass through the nucleus. The predicted ground state therefore carries angular momentum $\hbar$, whereas the hydrogen ground state has none; wave mechanics gives $L^2=\ell(\ell+1)\hbar^2$ with $\ell=0,\ldots,n-1$. Einstein pointed out in 1917 that the rule can be stated without special coordinates, as conditions on loops around invariant tori in phase space. Motion without such tori cannot be quantized this way. The three-body motion of helium has no such structure, and orbit models failed to reproduce its ionization energy of 24.6 eV. The anomalous Zeeman effect demanded half-integer quantum numbers, later traced to electron spin. Line intensities lay outside the rule, and their calculation led Heisenberg to matrix mechanics in 1925."),
        ("p", r"The corrected rule survives as the leading term of semiclassical WKB theory. It remains accurate for highly excited Rydberg states and for the vibrational levels of molecules. The quantum harmonic oscillator and the angular momentum operator give the exact results that replaced the orbit picture."),
    ],
    "quantum_mechanics": [
        ("p", r"A beam of silver atoms passing an inhomogeneous magnet splits into two spots instead of a continuous smear. Stern and Gerlach observed this in 1922. Sequences of such magnets display most of the structure of quantum mechanics in one experiment."),
        ("p", r"The magnetic moment of a silver atom comes from one unpaired electron spin. Its spin state is a unit vector $|\psi\rangle=\alpha|\uparrow\rangle+\beta|\downarrow\rangle$ in a two-dimensional complex space. A magnet with its field gradient along $z$ sends the atom up or down with probabilities $|\alpha|^2$ and $|\beta|^2$. Blocking the lower beam prepares $|\uparrow\rangle$: every atom of the upper beam goes up again in a second $z$ magnet."),
        ("p", r"A magnet tilted by an angle $\theta$ from $z$ in the $xz$ plane has its own pair of output states. The probability of each output is the squared modulus of an overlap, the Born rule:"),
        ("eq", r"""|{+}\mathbf n\rangle=\cos\tfrac\theta2|\uparrow\rangle+\sin\tfrac\theta2|\downarrow\rangle,\qquad
p({+}\mathbf n\,|\uparrow)=|\langle{+}\mathbf n|\uparrow\rangle|^2=\cos^2\tfrac\theta2."""),
        ("p", r"At $\theta=60^\circ$ three quarters of the atoms take the upper path; at $90^\circ$ half do. Single atoms arrive at random, and only the relative counts approach these values."),
        ("p", r"Now place a third magnet along $z$ after the tilted one. Suppose the path through the tilted magnet is recorded. The probabilities of the two paths then add, and atoms that entered as $|\uparrow\rangle$ leave as $|\downarrow\rangle$ with probability $\tfrac12\sin^2\theta$. If the two paths are recombined without any record, the amplitudes add instead. Completeness of the two output states then gives zero:"),
        ("eq", r"""p_{\rm recorded}(\downarrow)=\sum_\pm|\langle\downarrow|{\pm}\mathbf n\rangle|^2|\langle{\pm}\mathbf n|\uparrow\rangle|^2
=\tfrac12\sin^2\theta,\\
p_{\rm recombined}(\downarrow)=\Bigl|\sum_\pm\langle\downarrow|{\pm}\mathbf n\rangle\langle{\pm}\mathbf n|\uparrow\rangle\Bigr|^2=0."""),
        ("p", r"The difference between the two lines is set by whether any physical system keeps information about the path. Coherent recombination is demanding, because the two paths must overlap again in position and momentum. A full-loop Stern--Gerlach interferometer driven by the precise field of an atom chip has achieved it."),
        ("p", r"The spin components $S_z$ and $S_x$ are represented by noncommuting operators, $[S_z,S_x]=i\hbar S_y$. No preparation gives definite values to both. After the $x$ magnet selects its upper beam, a $z$ magnet again splits the atoms evenly. The commutator and the uncertainty principle treat this incompatibility quantitatively."),
        ("p", r"Between magnets the state evolves by the Schrödinger equation. In a uniform field $B$ along $z$, take $H=\omega S_z$ with $\omega$ proportional to $B$. A spin prepared along $x$ precesses about $z$, and the probability of finding it along $x$ oscillates:"),
        ("eq", r"""i\hbar\frac{d}{dt}|\psi\rangle=H|\psi\rangle,\qquad
p_{+x}(t)=\cos^2\frac{\omega t}{2}."""),
        ("p", r"For an electron spin, $\omega/2\pi$ equals 28.0 gigahertz for each tesla of field. The same rules extend to any system. States are unit vectors or density operators, and outcomes are eigenvalues of self-adjoint operators. Probabilities are squared overlaps, evolution between measurements is unitary, and composite systems use tensor products. The two spots appear only when the gradient separates the paths by more than the beam width. With weaker gradients the spots overlap, and the magnet performs an unsharp measurement of the kind discussed under observables."),
    ],
    "mathematical_formulation_of_quantum_mechanics": [
        ("p", r"A neutron spin rotated through a full turn points in its original direction again, but its state vector changes sign. In 1975 the groups of Rauch and of Werner observed this sign with neutron interferometers, in which one of two beams crossed a magnetic field. Predicting the measured intensity uses each rule of the standard formulation once."),
        ("p", r"The outcomes of a measurement correspond to mutually orthogonal projectors $P_a$ on a complex Hilbert space, with $\sum_aP_a=I$. Probabilities must add over orthogonal projectors, and the probability of $P_a$ must not depend on which other projectors complete the measurement. In dimension three or more, Gleason's theorem shows that every such assignment has the trace form, so the state is a density operator $\rho$:"),
        ("eq", r"""p(a)=\operatorname{Tr}(\rho P_a),\qquad \rho\geq0,\qquad \operatorname{Tr}\rho=1."""),
        ("p", r"In dimension two, additional assumptions are needed for the same conclusion. Reversible evolution must preserve probabilities and compose in time. If it is continuous, Stone's theorem gives a self-adjoint generator $H$, and the Schrödinger equation follows:"),
        ("eq", r"""U(t+s)=U(t)U(s),\qquad U(t)=e^{-iHt/\hbar},\qquad
\rho(t)=U(t)\rho\,U(t)^\dagger."""),
        ("p", r"A measurement that would repeat its result immediately changes the state to $P_a\rho P_a$ divided by its trace, the Lüders rule. If the outcome is not recorded, the state becomes the sum $\sum_aP_a\rho P_a$. Composite systems are described on tensor products of the component spaces."),
        ("p", r"In the interferometer a silicon crystal splits each neutron into two paths, $|1\rangle$ and $|2\rangle$, and later recombines them. The state space is the tensor product of this path space with the spin space $\mathbb C^2$. In path 2 a field $B$ along $z$ acts over a length $L$. For a neutron of speed $v$ and gyromagnetic ratio $\gamma_n$, the field Hamiltonian generates a spin rotation by the angle $\alpha=\gamma_nBL/v$:"),
        ("eq", r"""U(\alpha)=e^{-i\alpha\sigma_z/2},\qquad U(2\pi)=-I,\qquad U(4\pi)=I."""),
        ("p", r"Let $\rho_s$ be the incoming spin state and $\chi$ the phase difference between the paths without field. The trace rule, with the projector onto one output beam, gives the fraction of neutrons leaving that beam. For an unpolarized beam, $\rho_s=I/2$:"),
        ("eq", r"""I(\alpha)=\frac12\Bigl[1+\operatorname{Re}\bigl(e^{i\chi}\operatorname{Tr}\rho_sU(\alpha)\bigr)\Bigr]
=\frac12\Bigl[1+\cos\chi\,\cos\frac{\alpha}{2}\Bigr]."""),
        ("p", r"The interference term reverses its sign at $\alpha=2\pi$ and returns only at $\alpha=4\pi$. A single beam cannot show the sign, because $-I$ multiplies every spin state by the same factor. The unrotated path supplies the reference. Thermal neutrons of wavelength 0.18 nanometres move at about 2200 metres per second. A full turn then needs $BL=2\pi v/\gamma_n\approx7.5\times10^{-5}$ tesla metre, for example 7.5 millitesla over one centimetre. Both 1975 experiments found the intensity periodic in the field with this 4$\pi$ period. A spread of neutron speeds spreads $\alpha$ in proportion to its size, which lowers the contrast after many turns."),
        ("p", r"A measurement of the spin in path 2 changes the prediction. Its outcomes correspond to the projectors $|2\rangle\langle2|\otimes P_\pm$, completed by $|1\rangle\langle1|\otimes I$ for neutrons in path 1. The Lüders rule removes every term of the density operator that connects the two paths, so the interference term vanishes. The intensity becomes one half for every field and every $\chi$. The record shows which path was taken, whether or not anyone reads it."),
        ("p", r"The formulation does not state which interactions count as measurements; decoherence and the measurement problem address that physical question. Within its rules, it gives the probability of any sequence of outcomes. The half-angle in $U(\alpha)$ comes from the spin-1/2 representation of rotations described under the Pauli matrices."),
    ],
    "quantum_differential_calculus": [
        ("p", r"An electron hopping between neighbouring atoms of a crystal changes its position in steps of the lattice constant $a$. Its kinetic energy then involves differences of the wave function between sites, not derivatives. On such a lattice the product rule of ordinary calculus fails. Repairing it gives the simplest example of a quantum differential calculus."),
        ("p", r"The name comes from quantum groups and noncommutative geometry. It denotes a consistent algebra of functions and differentials, not the quantization of a mechanical system. Take the forward difference $\partial f(x)=[f(x+a)-f(x)]/a$. Expanding the difference of a product gives an extra term:"),
        ("eq", r"""\partial(fg)=(\partial f)\,g+f\,(\partial g)+a\,(\partial f)(\partial g)."""),
        ("p", r"The extra term vanishes as $a\to0$. It can be removed exactly at finite $a$ if the differential $dx$ no longer commutes with functions. Define $df=(\partial f)\,dx$ and require the ordinary Leibniz rule $d(fg)=(df)\,g+f\,dg$. Both are consistent only if moving a function across $dx$ shifts its argument:"),
        ("eq", r"""dx\,f(x)=f(x+a)\,dx,\qquad dx\,x-x\,dx=a\,dx."""),
        ("p", r"Functions on the lattice commute with each other, but differentials do not commute with them. This is a noncommutative differential calculus on a commutative algebra. Replacing the translation by a dilation gives Jackson's q-derivative, $D_qf(x)=[f(qx)-f(x)]/[(q-1)x]$, with $dx\,f(x)=f(qx)\,dx$. On powers it produces the q-numbers:"),
        ("eq", r"""D_qx^n=[n]_qx^{n-1},\qquad [n]_q=\frac{q^n-1}{q-1}=1+q+\cdots+q^{n-1}."""),
        ("p", r"The lattice calculus has a measurable consequence. The kinetic energy built from a forward and a backward difference has plane waves $e^{ikx}$ as eigenfunctions, with"),
        ("eq", r"""E(k)=\frac{2\hbar^2}{ma^2}\sin^2\frac{ka}{2}
=\frac{\hbar^2k^2}{2m}-\frac{\hbar^2a^2k^4}{24m}+\cdots"""),
        ("p", r"The group velocity $(\hbar/ma)\sin ka$ vanishes at the zone edge $k=\pi/a$. A constant force $F$ advances $k$ uniformly in time, so the velocity oscillates instead of growing. The period of this Bloch oscillation is $2\pi\hbar/(Fa)$. The same structure describes the lowest band of a deep optical lattice, with a hopping energy in place of $\hbar^2/(2ma^2)$. The period does not depend on the hopping. In a vertical lattice of spacing 266 nanometres, gravity gives strontium-88 atoms a Bloch frequency $mga/h$ of about 574 hertz. Bloch oscillations of strontium-88 in a vertical optical lattice have been followed for several seconds and used to measure gravity at micrometre scales. The single-band description holds while the force is too weak to drive transitions to the next band."),
        ("p", r"The q-calculus has a less direct physical status. For a q-deformed oscillator with $aa^\dagger-qa^\dagger a=1$, the operator $a^\dagger a$ has eigenvalues $[n]_q$, so its levels are unequally spaced. To first order in $q-1$ the spacing changes linearly with $n$, like the anharmonic term of a molecular vibrational ladder. Such fits are phenomenological. It is established that these calculi are consistent algebraic structures. Quantum-group symmetry also appears exactly in integrable models, such as the anisotropic XXZ spin chain with suitable boundary terms. Whether spacetime itself has a deformed differential structure at short distances is open, and no experiment has shown it."),
    ],
    "transformation_theory_quantum_mechanics": [
        ("p", r"Cold atoms released into a harmonic trap form, after a quarter of an oscillation period, an image of their initial momentum distribution. The explanation is a calculation of the kind introduced by Dirac and Jordan in 1927. Their transformation theory treats every prediction as a product of amplitudes between eigenstates of different observables."),
        ("p", r"The amplitude $\langle a|b\rangle$ is the transformation function between eigenstates of two observables. Its squared modulus is the probability of finding $a$ after preparing $b$. Amplitudes compose by summing over a complete set of intermediate eigenstates. Wave mechanics and matrix mechanics then differ only in which basis the same amplitudes are written:"),
        ("eq", r"""\langle a|c\rangle=\int db\,\langle a|b\rangle\langle b|c\rangle,\qquad
\psi(q)=\langle q|\psi\rangle,\qquad \widetilde\psi(p)=\langle p|\psi\rangle."""),
        ("p", r"The central transformation function connects position and momentum. Because $[Q,P]=i\hbar$, momentum generates translations, and in the position basis it acts as $-i\hbar\,\partial_q$. Applied to a momentum eigenstate this gives a first-order differential equation. Normalization to $\langle p|p'\rangle=\delta(p-p')$ fixes the constant:"),
        ("eq", r"""-i\hbar\,\partial_q\langle q|p\rangle=p\,\langle q|p\rangle
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\langle q|p\rangle=\frac{e^{ipq/\hbar}}{\sqrt{2\pi\hbar}}."""),
        ("p", r"The modulus is the same for every $q$ and $p$. A state of sharp momentum therefore has a uniform position distribution, and the converse also holds. The spin version is $|\langle{\pm}x|{\pm}z\rangle|^2=\tfrac12$: complete knowledge of $S_z$ leaves the outcomes of $S_x$ equally likely."),
        ("p", r"Time evolution is also a transformation, from positions at one time to positions at another. For a particle of mass $m$ in a harmonic trap of angular frequency $\omega$, the energy eigenfunctions are Hermite functions $h_n$, each acquiring the phase $e^{-i(n+1/2)\omega t}$. At the quarter period $t=\pi/(2\omega)$ this phase equals $e^{-i\pi/4}(-i)^n$. The Hermite functions are also eigenfunctions of the Fourier transform with eigenvalues $(-i)^n$. The quarter-period evolution is therefore the position-to-momentum transformation itself:"),
        ("eq", r"""\psi\!\left(x,\frac{\pi}{2\omega}\right)=e^{-i\pi/4}\sqrt{m\omega}\;\widetilde\psi(m\omega x),
\qquad |\psi|^2dx=|\widetilde\psi(p)|^2dp\ \ \text{at}\ p=m\omega x."""),
        ("p", r"For rubidium-87 in a trap with a frequency of 10 hertz, the quarter period is 25 milliseconds. A momentum of $\hbar$ times one inverse micrometre maps to a displacement of 11.6 micrometres. Experiments with cold gases use this mapping to measure momentum distributions. The image is faithful when interactions are negligible during the quarter period and the trap is harmonic over the size of the cloud; anharmonicity blurs it."),
        ("p", r"The same composition rule underlies the Fourier transform between representations and the far-field imaging of a freely expanding cloud. Dirac also noted that the transformation function for a short time interval behaves like $\exp(iS/\hbar)$, with $S$ the classical action. Feynman developed that observation into the path integral. Von Neumann later recast the theory in Hilbert space without delta-normalized states; rigged Hilbert spaces restore them as generalized eigenvectors."),
    ],
    "observable": [
        ("p", r"A spin-1 atom passing a Stern--Gerlach magnet leaves three spots, one for each value of $S_z$. An apparatus that recombines the two outer beams without recording which one each atom took asks a coarser question. Comparing the two apparatuses shows what an observable specifies."),
        ("p", r"An observable is a list of outcomes together with orthogonal projectors that sum to the identity. The operator collects them in its spectral decomposition, and the projectors give the probabilities:"),
        ("eq", r"""A=\sum_ia_iP_i,\qquad P_iP_j=\delta_{ij}P_i,\qquad \sum_iP_i=I,\qquad
p(a_i)=\operatorname{Tr}(\rho P_i)."""),
        ("p", r"The eigenvalues $a_i$ label detector outputs; the projectors carry the physics. The mean $\operatorname{Tr}(\rho A)$ and the moments $\operatorname{Tr}(\rho A^k)$ follow from the same probabilities. A function $f(A)=\sum_if(a_i)P_i$ relabels the outputs and merges those that receive the same label. After a recorded outcome with minimal disturbance, the state becomes $P_i\rho P_i$ divided by its trace."),
        ("p", r"For spin 1, $S_z=\hbar(P_{+1}-P_{-1})$, while merging the outer beams measures $S_z^2=\hbar^2(P_{+1}+P_{-1})$. Prepare $|\psi\rangle=(|{+}1\rangle+|{-}1\rangle)/\sqrt2$. A measurement of $S_z$ gives $\pm\hbar$ with probability one half each and leaves $|{+}1\rangle$ or $|{-}1\rangle$. A measurement of $S_z^2$ gives $\hbar^2$ with certainty and leaves $|\psi\rangle$ unchanged, including the relative phase of its components. A later experiment sensitive to that phase distinguishes the two apparatuses, even though one of them gave a certain result."),
        ("p", r"For spin 1 the three squared components commute with each other. They obey a sum rule:"),
        ("eq", r"""[S_x^2,S_y^2]=[S_y^2,S_z^2]=[S_z^2,S_x^2]=0,\qquad
S_x^2+S_y^2+S_z^2=2\hbar^2."""),
        ("p", r"One apparatus can therefore measure all three. Each has eigenvalues $0$ and $\hbar^2$, and the sum rule forces exactly one zero in every run. The state $|\psi\rangle$ above gives $S_x^2=\hbar^2$, $S_y^2=0$ and $S_z^2=\hbar^2$ with certainty. By contrast, $S_x$ and $S_z$ do not commute, and no apparatus measures both sharply. Commuting triples of this kind underlie the Kochen--Specker theorem discussed under quantum logic."),
        ("p", r"A real detector can assign an outcome to the wrong beam. Suppose a spin-1/2 analyzer reports the wrong spot with probability $\varepsilon$, for instance because the spots overlap. The report up then corresponds to the positive operator $E_+=(1-\varepsilon)P_\uparrow+\varepsilon P_\downarrow$, and"),
        ("eq", r"""E_\pm=\frac{I\pm\eta\,\sigma_z}{2},\qquad \eta=1-2\varepsilon,\qquad
p_\pm=\frac{1\pm\eta\,r_z}{2},"""),
        ("p", r"where $r_z$ is the $z$ component of the Bloch vector. These operators are not projectors, and together they form a positive-operator-valued measure. The mean outcome still determines $r_z$ after division by $\eta$, but no single run gives certainty when $\varepsilon>0$. For continuous spectra the projectors are assigned to intervals of outcomes, as described in spectral theory. Two devices measure the same observable when they assign the same operators to their outcomes, whatever their construction."),
    ],
    "self_adjoint_operator": [
        ("p", r"A charged particle circulates in a thin ring threaded by magnetic flux. Its momentum values shift with the flux, although the field vanishes where the particle moves. The shift enters through a boundary condition. That boundary condition is exactly what makes the momentum operator self-adjoint."),
        ("p", r"Let $P=-i\hbar\,d/dx$ act on wave functions on an interval $[0,L]$. Integration by parts leaves a boundary term:"),
        ("eq", r"""\langle\phi|P\psi\rangle-\langle P\phi|\psi\rangle
=-i\hbar\bigl[\phi^*(L)\psi(L)-\phi^*(0)\psi(0)\bigr]."""),
        ("p", r"On functions that vanish at both ends the term is zero, so $P$ is symmetric and its expectation values are real. It is not self-adjoint, however. Its adjoint acts on all differentiable functions without any boundary condition, and on that larger domain every $e^{ipx/\hbar}$ with complex $p$ is an eigenfunction. The spectrum of such an operator is not a set of real outcomes."),
        ("p", r"The deficiency indices measure the mismatch. Count the normalizable solutions of $P^\dagger\psi=\pm i\kappa\psi$ for a fixed $\kappa>0$; they are $e^{\mp\kappa x/\hbar}$. A symmetric operator has self-adjoint extensions only when the two counts $n_\pm$ are equal. The extensions are then labelled by unitary maps between the two solution spaces:"),
        ("eq", r"""[0,L]:\ (n_+,n_-)=(1,1),\qquad \psi(L)=e^{i\theta}\psi(0),\qquad
p_k=\frac{\hbar(2\pi k+\theta)}{L},\\
[0,\infty):\ (1,0),\ \text{no extension};\qquad
\mathbb R:\ (0,0),\ \text{unique}."""),
        ("p", r"On the interval each phase $\theta$ defines a different self-adjoint momentum with its own real spectrum, $k$ an integer. On a ring threaded by flux $\Phi$, a gauge transformation moves the vector potential into this boundary condition, with $\theta=2\pi\Phi/\Phi_0$ and $\Phi_0=h/e=4.14\times10^{-15}$ weber. The energies $p_k^2/2m$ are periodic in the flux with period $\Phi_0$; this is the Aharonov--Bohm effect. Persistent currents in mesoscopic metal rings show this flux period. In a single ring, disorder, temperature and the many electrons of a metal reduce the current amplitude, while the period $h/e$ remains."),
        ("p", r"On the half line the counts differ, and no self-adjoint momentum exists. A translation toward the wall would push probability through the wall, so no unitary translation group exists there. Stone's theorem then provides no self-adjoint generator. The momentum of a particle confined to $x>0$ can be measured only as a generalized observable."),
        ("p", r"The kinetic energy on the half line behaves differently, with indices $(1,1)$. Its extensions impose $\psi'(0)=-\psi(0)/b$ for real $b$, with the Dirichlet condition as the limit $b\to0$. For $b>0$ the function $e^{-x/b}$ is a bound state with energy $-\hbar^2/(2mb^2)$, describing a short-range attraction at the wall. On a finite box the kinetic energy has indices $(2,2)$, a family of conditions that includes the Dirichlet, Neumann and periodic cases compared in spectral theory."),
        ("p", r"Self-adjointness is required for two physical reasons. Only self-adjoint operators have spectral decompositions with real outcomes, and only they generate unitary evolution. Symmetry alone, real expectation values on a restricted domain, provides neither. The choice of domain is part of the physical description of the system, as in the treatments of observables and of the mathematical formulation."),
    ],
    "eigenvalues_and_eigenvectors": [
        ("p", r"Sodium vapour illuminated by light with two optical frequencies fluoresces brightly, except where the difference of the two frequencies matches the splitting of two ground levels. There the fluorescence drops sharply. Alzetta and coworkers observed such dark lines in 1976. They are explained by an eigenvector of the atom-light Hamiltonian that has no excited-state component."),
        ("p", r"An eigenvector of the Hamiltonian, $H|n\rangle=E_n|n\rangle$, changes in time only by a phase, so all of its probabilities stay constant. A general state is a sum of eigenvectors, each with its own phase. Expectation values therefore oscillate at the Bohr frequencies $(E_n-E_m)/\hbar$:"),
        ("eq", r"""|\psi(t)\rangle=\sum_nc_ne^{-iE_nt/\hbar}|n\rangle,\qquad
\langle A\rangle(t)=\sum_{m,n}c_m^*c_nA_{mn}\,e^{-i(E_n-E_m)t/\hbar}."""),
        ("p", r"Spectral lines appear at differences of eigenvalues for this reason. For a Hermitian $H$, evaluate $\langle m|H|n\rangle$ by acting to the right and to the left. The two results give $(E_n-E_m^*)\langle m|n\rangle=0$. Setting $m=n$ shows that eigenvalues are real, and distinct eigenvalues have orthogonal eigenvectors. In finite dimensions the eigenvalues are the roots of $\det(H-EI)=0$."),
        ("p", r"In the dark-resonance experiment, two ground levels $|1\rangle$ and $|2\rangle$ couple to one excited level $|e\rangle$. Two light fields drive the transitions with Rabi frequencies $\Omega_p$ and $\Omega_c$. In a frame rotating with the fields, within the rotating-wave approximation and with both fields on resonance, the Hamiltonian in the basis $(|1\rangle,|2\rangle,|e\rangle)$ is"),
        ("eq", r"""H=\frac\hbar2\begin{pmatrix}0&0&\Omega_p\\0&0&\Omega_c\\ \Omega_p&\Omega_c&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\det(H-EI)=-E\left(E^2-\frac{\hbar^2\Omega^2}{4}\right),"""),
        ("p", r"with $\Omega^2=\Omega_p^2+\Omega_c^2$. The eigenvalues are $0$ and $\pm\hbar\Omega/2$. The eigenvector with eigenvalue zero is"),
        ("eq", r"""|D\rangle=\frac{\Omega_c|1\rangle-\Omega_p|2\rangle}{\Omega}."""),
        ("p", r"Its excited-state amplitude vanishes because the two excitation paths interfere destructively. An atom in $|D\rangle$ neither absorbs nor fluoresces. Spontaneous decay from $|e\rangle$ returns atoms to the ground levels at random, and atoms accumulate in $|D\rangle$. This is coherent population trapping. The state remains an exact eigenvector for any common detuning of the two fields, as long as their frequency difference matches the ground splitting. A mismatch $\delta$ couples $|D\rangle$ to the other states in proportion to $\delta$, and fluorescence returns."),
        ("p", r"The width of the dark resonance is set by the decay of the coherence between the ground levels and by power broadening. It can be far narrower than the optical linewidth. Compact atomic clocks use this resonance to lock a laser frequency difference to the cesium ground-state splitting of 9.192631770 gigahertz."),
        ("p", r"Eigenvalues alone would not explain the effect: the spectrum $0,\pm\hbar\Omega/2$ says nothing about fluorescence. The prediction depends on the composition of the eigenvector. When eigenvalues are degenerate, the eigenvectors are not unique, and a perturbation selects the physically relevant combinations, as in perturbation theory. The two-state quantum system gives the simplest case of the same calculation."),
    ],
    "operator_theory": [
        ("p", r"The energy of an electron in an atom can take arbitrarily large values, while a spin-1/2 component never exceeds $\hbar/2$ in magnitude. Operator theory distinguishes these cases as unbounded and bounded operators. The distinction decides which mathematical statements hold, and it explains why a hydrogen atom does not collapse."),
        ("p", r"The norm $\|A\|$ is the largest factor by which $A$ can stretch a unit vector. For an observable it is the largest magnitude of an outcome. A bounded operator acts on every state. An unbounded symmetric operator, such as the kinetic energy, cannot act on every state. By the Hellinger--Toeplitz theorem, a symmetric operator defined everywhere is bounded."),
        ("p", r"Position and momentum cannot both be bounded. Suppose $[A,B]=cI$ with $c\neq0$. Induction gives $[A,B^n]=ncB^{n-1}$. Taking norms of both sides yields"),
        ("eq", r"""n|c|\,\|B^{n-1}\|\leq2\|A\|\,\|B\|\,\|B^{n-1}\|."""),
        ("p", r"No power of $B$ vanishes, because $B^{n-1}=0$ would force $B^{n-2}=0$ and so on down to $I=0$. Dividing by $\|B^{n-1}\|$ gives $n\leq2\|A\|\|B\|/|c|$ for every $n$, which is impossible. This argument of Wielandt holds in infinite dimensions; the finite-dimensional trace argument appears under the canonical commutation relation. Canonical pairs therefore require unbounded operators, and with them stated domains."),
        ("p", r"In hydrogen, $H=p^2/2m-k/r$ with $k=e^2/(4\pi\varepsilon_0)$. The Coulomb term is unbounded below, and a classical electron would radiate and spiral into the nucleus. The kinetic energy controls the singularity through Hardy's inequality, valid for wave functions in three dimensions:"),
        ("eq", r"""\int|\nabla\psi|^2d^3x\geq\frac14\int\frac{|\psi|^2}{r^2}\,d^3x
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
H\geq\frac{\hbar^2}{8mr^2}-\frac{k}{r}\geq-\frac{2mk^2}{\hbar^2}."""),
        ("p", r"The last step minimizes the function of $r$ at $r=\hbar^2/(4mk)$. The bound equals four rydbergs below zero, $-54.4$ eV, compared with the exact ground energy of $-13.6$ eV. It is not sharp, but it proves that the energy is bounded below. Compressing the wave function raises its kinetic energy faster than the Coulomb attraction lowers its potential energy."),
        ("p", r"The same comparison shows that the Coulomb term is small relative to the kinetic energy. The Kato--Rellich theorem then makes $H$ self-adjoint on the domain of the kinetic energy, as Kato proved for atoms in 1951. The evolution $e^{-iHt/\hbar}$ is therefore uniquely defined."),
        ("p", r"The argument fails for stronger singularities. For $V=-g/r^2$, Hardy's inequality gives $H\geq0$ when $g\leq\hbar^2/(8m)$. For larger $g$ the energy is unbounded below, and the Hamiltonian needs an extra condition at the origin to define the dynamics. A point electric dipole produces an inverse-square potential with angular dependence. It can bind an electron only above a critical moment of about 1.63 debye. The relativistic kinetic energy $\sqrt{p^2c^2+m^2c^4}$ grows only linearly with momentum. A Coulomb potential of nuclear charge $Ze$ then keeps the energy bounded below only for $Z\alpha\leq2/\pi$. Self-adjointness and spectral theory build on these estimates."),
    ],
    "quantum_logic": [
        ("p", r"Two crossed polarizers block all light. A third polarizer inserted between them at 45 degrees lets one eighth of unpolarized light through. The propositions tested by polarizers do not combine like the propositions of classical logic, and quantum logic describes how they do combine."),
        ("p", r"The proposition that a photon passes a polarizer at angle $\theta$ corresponds to the projector $P_\theta$ onto that polarization. Its negation is the orthogonal polarization. The conjunction of two propositions is certain exactly when both are certain; it corresponds to the intersection of their subspaces. The disjunction corresponds to the smallest closed subspace containing both. Birkhoff and von Neumann showed in 1936 that these operations form a lattice:"),
        ("eq", r"""a\wedge b\leftrightarrow \operatorname{ran}P_a\cap\operatorname{ran}P_b,\qquad
a\vee b\leftrightarrow \overline{\operatorname{ran}P_a+\operatorname{ran}P_b},\qquad
\neg a\leftrightarrow I-P_a."""),
        ("p", r"No polarization passes the $0^\circ$ and $45^\circ$ polarizers with certainty, so their conjunction is the zero subspace. Yet light does pass the sequence of three polarizers. A sequence of filters is not a conjunction. Von Neumann showed that the conjunction is the limit of infinitely many alternations of the two projectors. For $0^\circ$ and $45^\circ$, each further polarizer of an alternating stack transmits half the light:"),
        ("eq", r"""(P_aP_b)^n\longrightarrow P_{a\wedge b}\quad(n\to\infty),\qquad
I_m=I_1\cos^{2m}45^\circ=2^{-m}I_1."""),
        ("p", r"The intensity after $m$ further polarizers tends to zero, the empty conjunction. For commuting projectors the product $P_aP_b$ is already the conjunction."),
        ("p", r"The distributive law fails. Let $a$ be horizontal polarization, $b$ the diagonal one and $\neg b$ the antidiagonal one. The disjunction $b\vee\neg b$ is the whole space, while neither $a\wedge b$ nor $a\wedge\neg b$ contains a nonzero vector:"),
        ("eq", r"""a\wedge(b\vee\neg b)=a\neq0=(a\wedge b)\vee(a\wedge\neg b)."""),
        ("p", r"Physically, a diagonal analyzer always gives one of its two outcomes for a horizontal photon, but neither outcome is certain in advance. No polarization makes both horizontal and diagonal certain. A description that assigned every photon a definite answer to both questions would satisfy the distributive law."),
        ("p", r"A weaker law remains, the orthomodular law: if $a\leq b$ then $b=a\vee(b\wedge\neg a)$. Within any set of mutually commuting projectors the lattice is Boolean, so classical logic holds for compatible propositions. The failures appear only across incompatible measurements. The Kochen--Specker theorem sharpens this. In dimension three or more, no assignment of true or false to all projectors makes exactly one member of every complete orthogonal set true. Kirchmair and coworkers tested a state-independent form with trapped ions in 2009, measuring triples of commuting two-qubit observables. The measured correlations exceeded the bound obeyed by any such assignment."),
        ("p", r"The lattice structure, Gleason's theorem and the Kochen--Specker theorem are established mathematics with tested consequences. The proposal that this lattice should replace classical logic in reasoning, argued by Putnam in 1968, is not generally accepted. Reasoning about experiments uses ordinary logic, while the lattice describes the propositions that experiments test. Projectors also generate the operator algebras discussed under von Neumann algebras."),
    ],
    "von_neumann_algebra": [
        ("p", r"An experimenter who controls one of two entangled spins, or the field inside one region of space, can apply only some operations. The set of those operations is an algebra closed under products, adjoints and limits. Von Neumann algebras describe such sets. Their structure decides whether the subsystem has a density matrix and how thermal equilibrium can be defined."),
        ("p", r"The commutant $M'$ of a set $M$ of operators contains every operator that commutes with all of $M$. Von Neumann's bicommutant theorem states that an algebra containing the identity, closed under adjoints and under weak limits, equals its double commutant. For two spins the operations on $A$ and on $B$ are each other's commutants:"),
        ("eq", r"""M'=\{B:\ [A,B]=0\ \text{for all}\ A\in M\},\qquad M''=M,\\
\bigl(\mathcal B(\mathcal H_A)\otimes I\bigr)'=I\otimes\mathcal B(\mathcal H_B)."""),
        ("p", r"The centre $M\cap M'$ contains operators that commute with everything in the algebra. If it holds more than multiples of the identity, the states split into sectors with no coherent superpositions between them. Total electric charge separates sectors in this way."),
        ("p", r"Algebras with trivial centre are called factors, and they come in types. A subsystem of finitely many degrees of freedom gives type I, where every state is a density matrix $\rho_A$ with $\langle A\rangle=\operatorname{Tr}(\rho_AA)$. An infinite chain of Bell pairs gives, on one side, an algebra with a normalized trace but no minimal projector, of type $\mathrm{II}_1$. The algebra of a region in relativistic quantum field theory has type III, with no trace at all. There the entanglement entropy of a region diverges unless a short-distance cutoff is imposed."),
        ("p", r"Thermal equilibrium can be stated without a density matrix. For a finite system, the Gibbs state $\rho=e^{-\beta H}/Z$ with $\beta=1/k_BT$ satisfies the Kubo--Martin--Schwinger condition. It follows from cyclicity of the trace, with $e^{-\beta H}$ acting as an evolution in imaginary time:"),
        ("eq", r"""\langle A(t)B\rangle_\beta=\langle B\,A(t+i\hbar\beta)\rangle_\beta,\qquad
A(t)=e^{iHt/\hbar}Ae^{-iHt/\hbar}."""),
        ("p", r"In an infinite system $e^{-\beta H}$ has no trace, but the condition still makes sense on the algebra and defines equilibrium. Several solutions at the same temperature describe coexisting phases, such as the two magnetizations of a ferromagnet. Tomita--Takesaki theory shows that every faithful normal state of a von Neumann algebra satisfies this condition for a flow of its own, the modular flow."),
        ("p", r"The condition has a measured consequence. Fourier transforming it for the correlation function of an observable gives detailed balance, with $\omega>0$ the energy taken up by the system:"),
        ("eq", r"""S(\omega)=\int dt\,e^{i\omega t}\langle A(t)A(0)\rangle_\beta,\qquad
S(-\omega)=e^{-\beta\hbar\omega}S(\omega)."""),
        ("p", r"In Raman scattering from silicon at 300 kelvin, the optical phonon at 520 inverse centimetres gives an anti-Stokes to Stokes ratio of $e^{-2.49}\approx0.083$. This value holds after correction for the frequency dependence of scattering. The ratio serves as a contactless thermometer. The Bisognano--Wichmann theorem applies the same condition to the vacuum restricted to a wedge of spacetime, with boosts as the flow. It predicts the Unruh temperature $\hbar a/(2\pi ck_B)$, about 0.41 kelvin for an acceleration of $10^{20}$ metres per second squared. The theorem is established; a direct observation of the Unruh effect is still lacking. Quantum statistical mechanics uses the finite-system form of the same condition."),
    ],
    "commutator": [
        ("p", r"Rotating a spin by a small angle about $x$, then about $y$, and then reversing both rotations in the same order leaves it rotated about $z$. The net rotation is of second order in the angle. Its size and axis are fixed by the commutator of the two generators, not by either rotation alone."),
        ("p", r"The commutator $[A,B]=AB-BA$ compares the two orders of applying two operators. For generators $X$ and $Y$ of transformations, the Baker--Campbell--Hausdorff formula gives $e^Xe^Y=\exp(X+Y+\tfrac12[X,Y]+\cdots)$. The four-step loop therefore has the commutator as its leading term. For spin rotations $R_{\mathbf n}(\varepsilon)=e^{-i\varepsilon\,\mathbf n\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma/2}$, with $[\sigma_x,\sigma_y]=2i\sigma_z$, this gives"),
        ("eq", r"""e^Xe^Ye^{-X}e^{-Y}=e^{[X,Y]+O(3)},\\
R_x(\varepsilon)R_y(\varepsilon)R_x(-\varepsilon)R_y(-\varepsilon)=R_z(\varepsilon^2)+O(\varepsilon^3)."""),
        ("p", r"The operator product acts from right to left. For $\varepsilon=0.1$ radian the exact product is a rotation by 0.0100 radian about an axis tilted about four degrees from $z$. Reversing the orientation of the loop reverses the sense of the rotation. Pulse sequences in magnetic resonance generate rotations about $z$ from $x$ and $y$ pulses alone. More generally, repeated commutators of the available Hamiltonians determine which operations a set of controls can reach."),
        ("p", r"The same term measures the error of splitting an evolution. Approximating $e^{-i(A+B)t/\hbar}$ by alternating $A$ and $B$ gives"),
        ("eq", r"""e^{-iAt/\hbar}e^{-iBt/\hbar}
=\exp\!\left(-\frac{i(A+B)t}{\hbar}-\frac{t^2}{2\hbar^2}[A,B]+\cdots\right)."""),
        ("p", r"Divided into $n$ steps, the error is bounded to leading order by $t^2\|[A,B]\|/(2n\hbar^2)$, which sets the cost of digital quantum simulation. Commuting terms can be split without error."),
        ("p", r"Two identities make commutators computable. The Leibniz rule $[A,BC]=[A,B]C+B[A,C]$ reduces composite expressions to simple ones, and the Jacobi identity relates commutators of commutators. For example, $[L_z,x]=i\hbar y$ and $[L_z,y]=-i\hbar x$ give $[L_z,x^2+y^2]=0$. The generator of rotations about $z$ therefore commutes with any potential that depends on $x$ and $y$ only through $x^2+y^2$."),
        ("p", r"Commuting Hermitian operators share an eigenbasis. If $[A,B]=0$ and $A|a\rangle=a|a\rangle$, then $A(B|a\rangle)=aB|a\rangle$, so $B$ maps each eigenspace of $A$ into itself and can be diagonalized there. The labels $n,\ell,m$ of hydrogen come from the commuting set $H$, $L^2$, $L_z$. A measurement of $L_x$ on such an eigenstate preserves $n$ and $\ell$, because $L_x$ also commutes with $H$ and $L^2$, but it generally leaves no definite value of $m$. A nonzero commutator excludes a complete common eigenbasis but not every common eigenvector: all three components of $L$ annihilate an $\ell=0$ state."),
        ("p", r"For unbounded operators, commuting on a common dense domain does not guarantee that the spectral projectors commute. Nelson constructed operators with that property whose unitary groups fail to commute. Compatibility is therefore defined through the projectors or the unitary groups. The uncertainty principle turns a nonzero commutator into a bound on spreads, and the Heisenberg picture uses $[H,A]$ for the time dependence of $A$."),
    ],
    "uncertainty_principle": [
        ("p", r"A beam of molecules passing a slit spreads as the slit is narrowed. Nairz, Arndt and Zeilinger measured this broadening for C70 fullerene molecules at a temperature of 900 kelvin. The slit width times the momentum transfer to the first diffraction minimum equalled Planck's constant $h$. A narrower position distribution forces a wider momentum distribution, a statement that holds for every preparation, not only for a disturbed one."),
        ("p", r"For observables $A$ and $B$ and a normalized state $\psi$, define $f=(A-\langle A\rangle)\psi$ and $g=(B-\langle B\rangle)\psi$. The variances are $\sigma_A^2=\langle f|f\rangle$ and $\sigma_B^2=\langle g|g\rangle$. The overlap $\langle f|g\rangle$ has a real part equal to the covariance and an imaginary part equal to $\langle[A,B]\rangle/2i$. The Cauchy--Schwarz inequality $|\langle f|g\rangle|^2\leq\langle f|f\rangle\langle g|g\rangle$ then gives the Robertson--Schrödinger relation:"),
        ("eq", r"""\sigma_A^2\sigma_B^2\geq
\left(\tfrac12\langle\{A,B\}\rangle-\langle A\rangle\langle B\rangle\right)^2
+\left|\frac{\langle[A,B]\rangle}{2i}\right|^2."""),
        ("p", r"Dropping the covariance gives Robertson's form $\sigma_A\sigma_B\geq\tfrac12|\langle[A,B]\rangle|$. For position and momentum it becomes $\hbar/2$, as derived under the canonical commutation relation."),
        ("p", r"The bound depends on the state. For a spin 1/2 with Bloch vector $\mathbf r$, the spin components give $\sigma_{S_x}^2=\tfrac{\hbar^2}{4}(1-r_x^2)$, $\sigma_{S_y}^2=\tfrac{\hbar^2}{4}(1-r_y^2)$ and $\langle[S_x,S_y]\rangle=i\hbar^2r_z/2$. For a spin along $x$ the Robertson bound is zero, although $S_y$ is maximally spread. Pure states satisfy the full relation with equality, because in two dimensions $f$ and $g$ are both orthogonal to $\psi$ and hence parallel:"),
        ("eq", r"""(1-r_x^2)(1-r_y^2)=r_z^2+r_x^2r_y^2\qquad (|\mathbf r|=1)."""),
        ("p", r"An entropic form removes the state dependence. Maassen and Uffink proved that the Shannon entropies of two measurements obey $H(A)+H(B)\geq-2\log_2c$, where $c$ is the largest overlap between their eigenvectors. For $S_z$ and $S_x$ of a spin 1/2, $c=1/\sqrt2$:"),
        ("eq", r"""H(Z)+H(X)\geq1\ \text{bit}."""),
        ("p", r"A $z$ eigenstate attains the bound with $H(Z)=0$ and $H(X)=1$. This form enters the security proofs of quantum key distribution."),
        ("p", r"Standard deviations can also fail to describe a measurement. A slit of width $w$ with uniform amplitude has position spread $w/\sqrt{12}$. Its momentum amplitude is proportional to $\sin(pw/2\hbar)/p$, whose variance is infinite, so the variance relation holds trivially and says nothing. The central diffraction peak, between zeros at $p=\pm h/w$, contains 90.3 percent of the probability. The product of slit width and peak half-width is $h$, the relation found in the fullerene experiment. A width that contains a fixed fraction of the probability is therefore the meaningful measure of spread here."),
        ("p", r"These relations concern spreads in identically prepared ensembles. Relations between the error of one measurement and the disturbance it causes to another are separate inequalities with their own definitions. One such relation, proposed by Ozawa, has been tested with successive measurements of neutron spins. The commutator and the Fourier transform give the operator and wave pictures of the same incompatibility."),
    ],
}

